  "id": "01",
  "name": "Crop and animal production, hunting and related service activities",
  "section": "A",
  "summary": "Agricultural producers describe AI mainly as own-use precision agriculture: satellite and drone imagery, yield prediction, autonomous machinery, camera-guided weeding and spraying, automated grading and livestock monitoring, usually framed as an answer to labour shortages and input costs. Plantation, greenhouse and livestock groups add mechanisation, climate-control and animal-health monitoring programmes, and seed and breeding companies write about predictive breeding and phenotyping. AI is rarely a demand driver here; farm-data ownership, rural connectivity and drone rules are the sector-specific risk angles.",
